x86_64-linux  clang emits __cxa_free_exception and cxx1 does not. It is the
other half of __cxa_allocate_exception: the memory is handed back if the
object cannot be got into it - if the copy that stores the thrown value into
that memory throws, the allocation must be freed before the new exception
leaves, and clang wraps the store in a cleanup region that calls it.

cxx1 emits allocate, store, throw with nothing around the store, so an
exception raised by that copy leaks the allocation. It is a real gap and not a
naming difference: it needs a cleanup region covering the window between the
allocate and the throw, which is the same machinery the destructible-local-
and-try limit is waiting on. Recorded here rather than papered over, and the
only difference in this case - every other name agrees on both Itanium
targets, and arm64-darwin agrees on all of them.
